tions, including love, hate, fear, shame, guilt, joy, disgust, and empathy, as shown by animals’ emotional lives.","confidence":0.78},"academicOrientation":{"score":35,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.72,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Jan van Hooff","confidence":0.62}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"New York Times","confidence":0.38}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"0227e369f42c14b08b62cf78ca6d2ce54e61673d71839eeef35fbc347f6b5d4d","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Science","secondarySubjects":["Nature & Environment"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores animal emotions and their implications for understanding both animal and human experiences, thus fitting squarely within the domain of Science, with a secondary focus on Nature & Environment due to its exploration of animal behavior.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"c362325ddb7a45694fe2d52d0daa3242fb047a1a094d75bf15970f95057f24fb","summary":"A New York Times Bestseller and winner of the PEN / E. O. Wilson Literary Science Writing Award Primatologist Frans de Waal explores the fascinating world of animal and human emotions. Frans de Waal has spent four decades at the forefront of animal research. Following up on the best-selling Are We Smart Enough to Know How Smart Animals Are?, which investigated animal intelligence, Mama’s Last Hug delivers a fascinating exploration of the rich emotional lives of animals. Mama’s Last Hug begins with the death of Mama, a chimpanzee matriarch who formed a deep bond with biologist Jan van Hooff. When Mama was dying, van Hooff took the unusual step of visiting her in her night cage for a last hug. Their goodbyes were filmed and went viral. Millions of people were deeply moved by the way Mama embraced the professor, welcoming him with a big smile while reassuring him by patting his neck, in a gesture often considered typically human but that is in fact common to all primates. This story and others like it form the core of de Waal’s argument, showing that humans are not the only species with the capacity for love, hate, fear, shame, guilt, joy, disgust, and empathy. De Waal discusses facial expressions, the emotions behind human politics, the illusion of free will, animal sentience, and, of course, Mama’s life and death.","publicationYear":2019,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Science","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-mama-s-last-hug-animal-emotions-and-what-they-tell-us-about-ourselves-frans-de-waal"}],"displaySummary":"A on the best-selling Are We Smart Enough to Know How Smart Animals Are?, which investigated animal intelligence, Mama’s Last Hug delivers a fascinating exploration of the rich emotional lives of animals. Mama’s Last Hug begins with the death of Mama, a chimpanzee matriarch who formed a deep bond with biologist Jan van Hooff. When Mama was dying, van Hooff took the unusual step of visiting her in her night cage for a last hug. Their goodbyes were filmed and went viral. Millions of people were deeply moved by the way Mama embraced the professor, welcoming him with a big smile while reassuring him by patting his neck, in a gesture often considered typically human but that is in fact common to all primates. This story and others like it form the core of de Waal’s argument, showing that humans are not the only species with the capacity for love, hate, fear, shame, guilt, joy, disgust, and empathy. De Waal discusses facial expressions, the emotions behind human politics, the illusion of free will, animal sentience, and, of course, Mama’s life and death.","primaryTopic":"Natural History & Animals","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Science","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Science","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Science","